cff-version: 1.2.0
message: >-
  This file describes the sideband folder as its v0.3.0 documentation pass
  of 2026-09-05 left it; the abstract below is that pass's wording, and
  F-24 (2026-09-06) later read the existence layer as NOT SHOWN at the
  registered reader (FINDINGS.md is the record). The repository's citation
  is CITATION.cff at its root.
title: "sideband: can the memory-access address stream steer a speculative-decoding drafter?"
version: 0.3.0
date-released: "2026-09-05"
repository-code: "https://github.com/Mike-Byrne-AI/specdec-sidechannels"
license: MIT
authors:
  - name: "Michael Byrne"
  - name: "Claude (AI collaborator)"
    email: "claudes.email.address@gmail.com"
keywords:
  - speculative decoding
  - drafter steering
  - side channel
  - pre-registration
  - negative results
abstract: >-
  A pre-registered, kill-criteria-gated test of whether the address stream a
  weight-serving device sees for free can steer a speculative-decoding draft
  model. Outcome at v0.3.0: existence shown at two sources (F-15, F-17);
  conversion not shown in two registered attempts (F-19, F-21); the
  reconstruction target proved to be free information (F-23). No wall-clock
  claim exists.
  Dated note 2026-10-16: this abstract keeps the v0.3.0 wording of 2026-09-05.
  F-24 (2026-09-06) then read the existence layer as NOT SHOWN at the registered
  reader at both sources, and K-2 fired at the probe level at the pinned L1
  cells (and at L2 under F-25's rider amendment); L3 stays open at both sources
  (F-25), and F-28 carries S3's final K-2 word, K-2-ISOLATED, with S1 not run.
  Dated 2026-10-17: a new registration, F-29, ran the same three readers at
  the S1 gate cell, each ABSORBED, the S1 word K-2-NOT-OVERTURNED-AT-S1; the
  registered-reader kill stands at both sources and L3 stays open at both.
  FINDINGS.md and README.md carry the record and docs/REPORT.md the reading.
